    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(6))  # F0..F5\n    customers = list(range(16))  # C0..C15\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(29, 54), (95, 80), (61, 28), (9, 50), (10, 50), (28, 66)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(18, 52), (97, 75), (35, 60), (82, 52), (74, 12), (60, 58), (55, 90), (28, 52), (75, 70), (94, 49), (3, 72), (75, 19), (55, 23), (67, 79), (72, 6), (61, 3)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [81.0, 186.0, 114.0, 173.0, 141.0, 174.0]\n    customer_demands = [19.0, 33.0, 24.0, 27.0, 39.0, 33.0, 15.0, 33.0, 44.0, 18.0, 18.0, 20.0, 15.0, 45.0, 10.0, 24.0]\n    fixed_costs = [492.0, 200.0, 278.0, 192.0, 264.0, 479.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse Location and Customer Fulfillment Decision\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution network. We have six candidate warehouse sites and sixteen customer destinations. The facility locations are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (29, 54)\n  - F1: (95, 80)\n  - F2: (61, 28)\n  - F3: (9, 50)\n  - F4: (10, 50)\n  - F5: (28, 66), and the customer locations are provided in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (18, 52)\n  - C1: (97, 75)\n  - C2: (35, 60)\n  - C3: (82, 52)\n  - C4: (74, 12)\n  - C5: (60, 58)\n  - C6: (55, 90)\n  - C7: (28, 52)\n  - C8: (75, 70)\n  - C9: (94, 49)\n  - C10: (3, 72)\n  - C11: (75, 19)\n  - C12: (55, 23)\n  - C13: (67, 79)\n  - C14: (72, 6)\n  - C15: (61, 3). Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, so shipping distance is based on the direct point-to-point separation rather than road mileage.\n\nOpening any warehouse will incur a fixed cost, summarized in [492.0, 200.0, 278.0, 192.0, 264.0, 479.0]. Each candidate site has a finite throughput limit as shown in [81.0, 186.0, 114.0, 173.0, 141.0, 174.0]. Each customer has a demand requirement that must be met in full, as listed in [19.0, 33.0, 24.0, 27.0, 39.0, 33.0, 15.0, 33.0, 44.0, 18.0, 18.0, 20.0, 15.0, 45.0, 10.0, 24.0]. Shipping costs scale with the distance traveled and the quantity we ship, using a transport rate of 0.5.\n\nOur business rules are straightforward:\n- A site must be opened before it can ship any product.\n- No open site should exceed its capacity.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n\nWithin those rules, we will determine which warehouses to open and how much volume to send from each open warehouse to each customer. The objective is to minimize our total cost\u2014combining the one-time cost to open warehouses and the ongoing transportation spend\u2014while honoring capacity limits and customer demand. The expected outcome is a clear recommendation on which sites to activate and a shipping plan that assigns customers to the most cost-effective mix of open warehouses.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse Location and Customer Fulfillment Decision\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution network. We have six candidate warehouse sites and sixteen customer destinations. The facility locations are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the customer locations are provided in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, so shipping distance is based on the direct point-to-point separation rather than road mileage.\n\nOpening any warehouse will incur a fixed cost, summarized in {FIXED_COSTS}. Each candidate site has a finite throughput limit as shown in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}. Each customer has a demand requirement that must be met in full, as listed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. Shipping costs scale with the distance traveled and the quantity we ship, using a transport rate of {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n\nOur business rules are straightforward:\n- A site must be opened before it can ship any product.\n- No open site should exceed its capacity.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n\nWithin those rules, we will determine which warehouses to open and how much volume to send from each open warehouse to each customer. The objective is to minimize our total cost\u2014combining the one-time cost to open warehouses and the ongoing transportation spend\u2014while honoring capacity limits and customer demand. The expected outcome is a clear recommendation on which sites to activate and a shipping plan that assigns customers to the most cost-effective mix of open warehouses."
}